Scheduling Concepts
Affinity y is an advanced scheduling feature that controls where pods are placed in a cluster.
we have pod affinity & node affinity
Node affinity
“Node affinity is a rule that restricts pods to run on specific nodes based on node labels.”
It allows Kubernetes to schedule pods on nodes that meet certain hardware, performance, or location requirements.
Types of Node Affinity
a) RequiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution (Hard Rule)
The pod must be scheduled on matching nodes, otherwise it stays pending.

_______________________
PreferredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution (Soft Rule)

Kubernetes tries to place the pod on preferred nodes but can choose others if needed.
________________________________________________________________
Pod affinity:
. Pod Affinity

Pod affinity is a scheduling rule, that ensures the pods are scheduled close to other pods with matching labels,
typically within the same node or availability zone to improve communication performance.
HARD Rule:
. Hard Rule (RequiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution)


A hard pod affinity rule is mandatory. Kubernetes must schedule the pod on a node that satisfies the affinity condition.
If no matching node exists, the pod remains in Pending state.
##If no node has a matching backend pod, scheduling fails.
____________-
Soft Rule (PreferredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution)
📌 Interview Definition

A soft pod affinity rule is a preference.
Kubernetes tries to schedule the pod according to the rule, but if it cannot, it still schedules the pod elsewhere.
Hard affinity rules can cause pods to stay pending if cluster resources are limited. 
In production, we mostly use soft affinity unless strict placement is required, to avoid availability issues.
________________________________________________
Anti-Affinity

Anti-affinity ensures pods are scheduled away from certain nodes or other pods. It is mainly used for high availability,
spreading replicas across different nodes or zones to avoid single points of failure.
