Resource Management
Requests

Requests define the minimum CPU and memory guaranteed to a container. Kubernetes uses requests for scheduling decisions.

Limits

Limits define the maximum resources a container can use. If exceeded, the container may be throttled (CPU) or killed (memory).

Autoscaling
HPA (Horizontal Pod Autoscaler)
4

HPA automatically scales the number of pod replicas based on metrics like CPU or custom metrics. 
It ensures applications handle variable load efficiently.

Cluster Autoscaler

Cluster Autoscaler automatically adds or removes nodes based on pod scheduling needs. If pods cannot be scheduled due to lack of resources, 
it adds nodes; if nodes are underutilized, it removes them.

VPA (Vertical Pod Autoscaler)

VPA automatically adjusts CPU and memory requests/limits of containers based on usage. It optimizes resource allocation without changing
pod count.

Node Maintenance Commands
Cordon

Cordon marks a node as unschedulable, preventing new pods from being placed on it, while existing pods continue running.

Uncordon

Uncordon makes the node schedulable again, allowing new pods to be assigned.

Drain

Drain safely evicts all pods from a node and prepares it for maintenance or shutdown, while respecting pod disruption budgets.

Scheduling Control
Taints

Taints are applied to nodes to repel certain pods. Pods without matching tolerations cannot be scheduled on that node.

Tolerations

Tolerations allow pods to run on nodes with matching taints. They enable controlled placement of special workloads.
